Support for Nested Randsequence
VCS supports randsequence inside randsequence with the -nested_randseq compile
time option.
Use the following option at compile time:
% vcs -sverilog <filename.sv> -nested_randseq
In vcs three step flow, use this option both at the vlogan and at vcs steps.
The following example shows the support for nested randsequence.
Example 96 Nested randsequence usage
bit topa;
topa = 0;
randsequence(main)
main : first|second done;
first : add |dec;
second : pop | push;
done : { topa = 1; };
add : { topa = 2; };
pop : { topa = 3; };
push : { topa = 4; };
dec : {
randsequence(main1) // Nested randsequence
main1 : first;
first : { topa = 5; };
endsequence };
if(!topa) $display("Failed");
else $display("Passed");
end
Support for Typed Constructor Call as an Argument to Task or
Function
VCS supports passing typed constructor call as an input type argument to task or function.

Example 97 test.v
int x;
function new(int v1);
this.x=v1;
class B extends A;
int y;
function new(int v2);
super.new(v2);
this.y=v2+1;
function void init(A a);
$display("%p",a);
init(B::new(5));
end
'{x:5, y:6}
The limitations of this feature are as follows:
• Typed constructor call is not supported in the default value of task or function
argument.
Support for Select of Unpacked Structure Array as an Argument
to Task or Function
VCS supports passing select of unpacked structure array (a structure which contains
unpacked array) as a ref type argument to task or function.

Example 98 test.v
typedef struct {int unsigned a; int unsigned b; int unsigned c;} st1_t;
typedef struct { st1_t x[2];} st2_t;
typedef struct {st2_t y[2]; } st3_t;
st3_t st_var;
set_y(st_var.y[0]);
$display(st_var.y[0]);
end
function set_y(ref st2_t e);
e.x[0] = '{9,8,7};
e.x[1] = '{9,8,7};
'{x:'{'{a:9, b:8, c:7}, '{a:9, b:8, c:7}} }
The limitation of this feature is as follows:
• Force on high-conn of a Function ref port is not supported.
Support for Function Returning Unpacked Structure in
Conditional Operator in a Continuous Assignment Statement
VCS supports function returning unpacked structure in conditional operator in RHS of a
continuous assignment statement.
The use of static or impure functions in the continuous assignment statement is not
recommended as it may result in synthesis simulation mismatches.
Example 99 test.sv
typedef struct {int intVar;} stDef;
int intVar1, intVar2;

stDef retVal;
bit trueOrFalse;
function stDef funcDef1(input int arg1);
stDef stObj1;
stObj1 = '{arg1};
return stObj1;
endfunction:funcDef1
function stDef funcDef2(input int arg2);
stDef stObj2;
stObj2 = '{arg2};
return stObj2;
endfunction:funcDef2
assign retVal = trueOrFalse ? funcDef1(intVar1) : funcDef2(intVar2);
always @(retVal.intVar)
$display("retVal.intVar at time %0t = %d",$time, retVal.intVar);
#1; intVar1 = 17; intVar2 = 25;
#1; trueOrFalse = 1;
#1; intVar1 = 01; intVar2 = 05;
end
Run the design using the following command:
% vcs -sverilog test.sv -full64 -R
retVal.intVar at time 1 = 25
retVal.intVar at time 2 = 17
retVal.intVar at time 3 = 1
Support for Built-in Method Which Returns Work Library Name
The built-in method get_worklib_name () returns the work library name of the given
class handle. The format of the string returned by the built-in method is as follows:
<lib name>.<package name>. <class name>
The syntax for get_worklib_name() is as follows:
string = <class_handle>.get_worklib_name();
where class_handle is the name of the class handle.

package pkg2;
class c12 #(int i = 24) extends pkg::cl;
int a2;

s = ci.get_worklib_name ();
$display ("%s", s);
ci = c12#(48)::new();
s = ci.get_worklib_name ();
$display ("%s", s);
end
synopsys_sim.setup file:
LIB_A : ./lib_a
LIB_B : ./lib_b
% vlogan -full64 -sverilog pkg1.v -work lib_a
% vlogan -full64 -sverilog pkg2.v -work lib_b
% vlogan -full64 -sverilog top.v -work work
% vcs top -full64 -R

\LIB_A.pkg .cl
\LIB_B.pkg2 .\cl2#(48)
Following are the limitations of this feature:
• If a parametrized class is defined in generate block, then the string returned by
get_worklib_name () for such class handle may not include generate block name in
the hierarchy.
• If a class is extended using the AOP construct, then the string returned by
get_worklib_name () for such class handle does not include library name or package
name in Partition Compile flow.
• If a class is defined inside the scope which contains any protected code, then the string
returned by get_worklib_name () for such class handle does not include the class
name.
• If the string output returned by the built-in method is equal to or more than 4095
characters, then the returned string is truncated and the last three characters in the
string are displayed as “…”.
Support for Function call in Clocking Block Hierarchical
Expression
VCS supports the user-defined function call in clocking block input hierarchical expression
as described in SystemVerilog LRM Std 1800™-2012 Section 14.5, Hierarchical
expressions.
As per LRM, any expression assigned to a signal in a clocking input or inout declaration
shall be an expression that would be legal for connection to a module’s input port.
The clocking block input hierarchical expression behaves the same as a continuous
assignment to the clockvar.
Example 101 test.sv
virtual class check;
static function bit func1(input logic t);
if($isunknown(t))

$display("Error at %0t: X/Z loss during 4-state to 2-state
assignment in testbench",$realtime);
return t;
endfunction : func1
endclass : check
logic logic_var;
bit bit_var;
logic clk=0;
clocking cb @(posedge clk);
input bit_var = check::func1(logic_var);
endclocking
always_ff @(cb) begin
$display("%0t - value of logic_var is %0b value of bit_var is %0b",
$realtime,logic_var,cb.bit_var);
case (logic_var)
1'bx: logic_var <= 'z;
1'bz: logic_var <= 0;
1'b0: logic_var <= 1;
default: logic_var <= 'x;
end
initial forever #5 clk = !clk;
initial #45 $finish;
% vlogan -full64 -sverilog test.sv
Error at 0: X/Z loss during 4-state to 2-state assignment in testbench

Error at 35: X/Z loss during 4-state to 2-state assignment in testbench
Following are the limitations of this feature:
• The function call is not allowed in clocking block output hierarchical expression.
• The hierarchical expression cannot contain multiple function calls.
• Function used in hierarchical expression cannot return dynamic data such as string,
class object, and so on.
• A function used in hierarchical expression cannot have arguments of dynamic type.

Using -ignore initializer_driver_checks Option for Ignoring Error
Related to Illegal Combination of Drivers
Multiple drivers can cause potential glitches in the simulation runs. The -ignore
initializer_driver_checks option ignores the initialization of the variable as a
procedural driver. It is recommended to verify that you intend to have multiple drivers for
this signal before suppressing the error.
The following is the syntax for -ignore initializer_driver_checks option:
% vcs -sverilog <file_name> -ignore initializer_driver_checks
Example 102 test.v
int x = 1;
assign x = 5;
initial #1 $display(x);
Run the above example without the -ignore initializer_driver_checks option as
The following error message is displayed.
Error-[ICPSD_INIT] Illegal combination of drivers
test.v, 2
Illegal combination of structural and procedural drivers.
Variable "x" is driven by an invalid combination of structural and
procedural drivers. Variables driven by a structural driver cannot have
any
other drivers.
This variable is declared at "test.v", 2: int x;
The first driver is at "test.v", 4: assign x = 5;
The second driver is at "test.v", 2: x = 1;
Use '-ignore initializer_driver_checks' to suppress this error
Run the above example with the -ignore initializer_driver_checks option as
% vcs -sverilog test.v -ignore initializer_driver_checks

The error message Error-[ICPSD_INIT] does not appear.
Support for Non-Singular types in $cast()
As per LRM 2023, chapter 6.24.2 updates, $cast has been extended to support non-
singular types, such as variable-sized arrays (dynamic arrays, associative arrays, queues),
unions, unpacked structures, unpacked arrays, and class handles. This change broadens
the applicability of $cast and aligns with the evolving needs of SystemVerilog usage.
%vcs –sverilog<file_name> -std=1800-2023
union {
int a;
bit [31:0] b;
} un_s, un_t;
un_s.b = 32'habcdef;
if ($cast(un_t,un_s))
$display("Pass : un_t.b= %h", un_t.b);
$display("Fail");
end
Pass : un_t.b = 00abcdef
• $cast involving functional call and streaming operator on source expressions are not
Support for Predefined Class weak_reference
VCS supports the predefined class weak_reference, as described in section 8.30 in
IEEE Std1800-2023.The weak_reference class is a built-in parameterized class in the

std package that allows weak references to objects, enabling access to an object without
preventing its garbage collection.
The following are the key features of weak_reference:
• Parameterized Class: The parameter type T must be a class type. Non-class types
results in a compiler error.
• Garbage Collection: If the memory management system determines that the referent is
weakly reachable, it clears all weak references to that referent atomically, allowing the
object to be garbage collected.
• Unique Instances: Each weak_reference instance is unique, even if they reference the
same object.
The following are the methods provided by the weak_reference:
• new(): Creates a new weak reference to a given object.
The prototype used for new() is the following:
function new(T referent);
• get(): Queries the referent object. Returns the referent if it has not been cleared;
otherwise, returns null.
The prototype used for get() is the following:
function T get();
• clear(): Clears the weak reference, making get() return null.
The prototype used for clear() is the following:
function void clear();
• get_id(): A static method that returns a deterministic identification value for the referent
object. If the object is null, it returns 0
The prototype used for get_id() is the following:
static function longint get_id(T obj);
