Support for Non-Dynamic Structure Member in Structural Context
This feature allows non-dynamic structure members to be used in structural contexts.
The structural contexts includes the following:
• Continuous assignments
• Port connections
• Force/release, assign/deassign, $monitor(),etc
• Other non procedural constructs that operate outside the procedural blocks
This is supported under the -blast_struct_with_dyn compile option.
Example 105 Continuous Assignment to Non-Dynamic Member of a Structure
string name;
byte data;
} st_t;
st_t st;
assign st.data = 8'hff;
$display("st.data: %h", st.data);
end

Behavior without the -blast_struct_with_dyn option:
%vcs -sverilog test1.sv
Error-[SV-NYI-RSUDD] Unsupported SystemVerilog feature
test1.v, 7
"assign st.data = 8'hff;"
Argument: st.data
Reference to a member of a structure containing dynamic data and/or used
in
dynamic arrays is not yet supported in non-procedural context.
Behavior with the -blast_struct_with_dyn option:
%vcs -sverilog test1.sv -blast_struct_with_dyn
st.data: ff
Example 106 Force/Release on Non-Dynamic Member of a Structure
class class_st;
bit grant;
class_st handle;
logic req;
}MEM;
module DUT;
logic clk;
bit grant;
MEM mem;
always #5 clk=~clk;
force mem.req = grant ? 1'b1 :1'b0 ;
release mem.req;
end
clk <= 1'b0;
grant = 1'b0;
#20 grant = 1'b1;
$display( "mem.req: %b",mem.req);
end
Behavior without the -blast_struct_with_dyn option:
%vcs -sverilog test2.sv

Error-[SV-NYI-RSUDD] Unsupported SystemVerilog feature
test2.v, 17
"force mem.req = (grant ? 1'b1 : 1'b0);"
Argument: mem.req
Reference to a member of a structure containing dynamic data and/or used
in
dynamic arrays is not yet supported in non-procedural context.

Error-[SV-NYI-RSUDD] Unsupported SystemVerilog feature
test2.v, 18
"release mem.req;"
Argument: mem.req
Reference to a member of a structure containing dynamic data and/or used
in
dynamic arrays is not yet supported in non-procedural context.
Behavior with the -blast_struct_with_dyn option:
%vcs -sverilog test2.sv -blast_struct_with_dyn
mem.req: 0
• The feature does not support structures that contain virtual interface objects.
• The feature is not supported when you use the -xlrm dyn_types_in_ca or -xlrm
nba_with_dyn_type options.
• The feature does not support when the structure has member initializer.
• The feature does not support structures containing rand or randc members.
Support for Array Map Methods
The array map() method in SystemVerilog allows you to create a new unpacked array by
applying a value expression to each element of an existing array. The method is useful for
transforming arrays, performing element-wise operations, and generating new arrays with
modified data types or values.
% vcs -sverilog <file_name>

Example 107 Array Map Addition and Comparison
int A[] = {1,2,3};
int B[] = {2,3,5};
int C[$];
int x = 1;
bit Compare[];
A = A.map() with (item + x);
$display(A);
C = A.map(a) with (a + B[a.index]);
$display(C);
Compare = A.map(a) with (a ==B[a.index]);
$display(Compare);
end
'{2, 3, 4}
'{4, 6, 9}
'{'h1, 'h1, 'h0}
Example 108 Associative Array Usage
int aa0[int];
string aa1[int];
aa1 = '{2:"hello", 3:"world", 4:"!"};
aa0 = aa1.map(item) with(item.len);
$display(aa0);
end
'{0x2:5, 0x3:5, 0x4:1}
• Array map methods do not support multi-dimensional array (MDA) nets.
• Array map methods are not supported in structure contexts.

Support for $timeunit and $timeprecision
According to the LRM 2023, Chapter 20.4.1, Timescale retrieval system functions, the
$timeunit and$timeprecision system functions help you find the time unit and time
precision for a design element.
You can use the following functions with or without an argument:
• If you do not give an argument, the function returns the time unit or precision of the
current scope.
• If you give an argument, the function returns the time unit or precision of the design
element you specify.
• If you use $unit as the argument, the function returns the time unit or precision of the
compilation unit.
• If you use $root as the argument, both functions return the simulation time unit.
The return value is an integer between 2 and -15 as shown in the following table:
Time Unit or Precision Unit Value
100s 2
10s 1
1s 0
100ms -1
10ms -2
1ms -3
100us -4
10us -5
1us -6
100ns -7
10ns -8
1ns -9
100ps -10

Time Unit or Precision Unit Value
10ps -12
1ps -12
100fs -13
10fs -14
1fs -15
Example 109 LRM compliant example
timeunit 100ps / 10fs;
module a_dat;
timeunit 1ms / 1us;
$display("simulation time unit: %0d, precision: %0d",
$timeunit($root), $timeprecision($root));
$display("compilation time unit: %0d, precision: %0d",
$timeunit($unit), $timeprecision($unit));
$display("a_dat time unit: %0d, precision: %0d",
$display("b_dat.c1 time unit: %0d, precision: %0d",
$timeunit(b_dat.c1), $timeprecision(b_dat.c1));
end
`timescale 10 fs / 1 fs
module b_dat;
c_dat c1 ();
`timescale 1 ns / 1 ns
module c_dat;
Simulation time unit: -15, precision: -15
Compilation time unit: -10, precision: -14
a_dat time unit: -3, precision -6
b_dat.c1 time unit: -9, precision: -9
