Supported Variations
VCS validates this feature across the following dimensions of variation:
• Subroutine type: task ref port and function ref port
• Interface array type: one-dimensional and multi-dimensional arrays; fixed and dynamic
arrays
• XMR forms: $unit, hierarchical module path, and nested virtual interface
• Element type: scalar, vector, array slice, unpacked array element, and related forms
• Eventing: events inside the task or function, outside the task or function, or both;
including timing control such as delays and fork-join
• Virtual interface lifetimes: assignment to a virtual interface before or after the task or
function call, and reassignment between calls

Support for Bit-Stream Casting to String
According to the SystemVerilog LRM Section 6.24.1, Cast operator, and Section 6.24.3,
Bit-stream casting, a data type can be changed by using a cast (') operation. For an
expression A cast to string using string'(A):
• When A is of string type, the cast behaves like a direct assignment (B = A ).
• When A is a bit-stream type, VCS packs A into a bit stream and then unpacks the bit
stream to string.
A bit-stream type consists of the following:
• Any integral, packed, or string type
• Unpacked arrays, structures, or classes of the preceding types
• Dynamically sized arrays (dynamic, associative, or queues) of any of the preceding
types
The expression string'(A) can be used anywhere a string type is expected, such as
input port high-connections, operands of string comparison operators, and the right-hand
side of nonblocking assignments, except in constraints.
VCS already supports bit-stream casting to string for integral types and classes. This
enhancement adds support for the remaining bit-stream types, including unpacked arrays
and structures. Casting a class to string retains the existing VCS behavior, which is not
fully LRM compliant, to avoid possible release-to-release (R2R) issues.
% vcs -sverilog <file_name>
Example 117 Unpacked String Array Cast to String
string fa[2] = '{"hello", " world"};
string s1;
string s2;
#1;
s1 = string'(fa);
#2;
s2 = {fa[0], fa[1]};
#1;
if (s1 == s2) $display("Test Passed :-)");
else $display("Test Failed :-)");

end
Test Passed :-)
