Supported Scenarios
The following are the supported scenarios:
• Randomization of single class object such as std::randomize(obj) is equivalent to
obj.randomize().
• Randomization of multiple class objects such as std::randomize(obj1, obj2) is
equivalent to the merge of the following two randomize calls:
obj1.randomize();
obj2.randomize();
• Randomization of class object and other non-class object variables such as
std::randomize(obj, a, b) is equivalent to the merge of the following two
randomize calls:
std::randomize(a, b);
The following scenarios are not supported, and an error message is issued:
• Array of class objects or array elements of class objects, including both fixed and
dynamic arrays. For example:
cls objs[3];
std::randomize(objs, objs[0]);
• Cross reference of class object. For example:
std::randomize(obj1.obj2);
Support for initial, extends, and final Specifiers in Constraints
The SystemVerilog 1800-2023 LRM introduces new specifiers :initial, :extends, and
:final.
The :initial specifier, when applied to a method or constraint block, marks it as eligible
to be executed or evaluated during the initialization phase of the simulation (time zero).

Support for initial, extends, and final Specifiers in Constraints
The :extends specifier explicitly declares that the method or constraint block in a derived
(child) class is an override of a definition provided in its base (parent) class.
The :final specifier guarantees that the method or constraint block cannot be
overridden, redefined, or modified by any class that derives from the current class.
VCS supports the :initial, :extends, and :final specifiers in the class constraint
declarations and external constraint blocks using the -std=1800-2023 compile option.
This feature helps to improve control over inheritance and overriding behavior.
The following is the syntax:
constraint_declaration ::= // from A.1.10
[ static ] constraint [ dynamic_override_specifiers ]
constraint_identifier constraint_block
constraint_block ::= {
{ constraint_block_item }
}
constraint_prototype ::=
[ constraint_prototype_qualifier ] [ static ] constraint
[ dynamic_override_specifiers ]
constraint_identifier ;
constraint_prototype_qualifier ::= extern | pure
extern_constraint_declaration ::=
[ static ] constraint [ dynamic_override_specifiers ] class_scope
constraint_identifier
constraint_block

dynamic_override_specifiers ::= [ initial_or_extends_specifier ]
[ final_specifier ]
initial_or_extends_specifier ::= : initial | : extends
final_specifier ::= : final
class base;
constraint cons2 {…}
endclass : base

class A extends base;
constraint :final :extends cons2{…}
// Correct usage: cons2 shall not be overridden in subclasses of A

constraint :final cons4{…}
// Correct usage: cons4 shall not be overridden in subclasses of A

constraint :extends cons5{…}
// Wrong usage: cons5 is not an override
endclass : A

Support for initial, extends, and final Specifiers in Constraints
class B extends A;
constraint cons2{…}
// Wrong usage: cons2 is specified final in A

constraint cons4{…}
// Wrong usage: A::cons4 is specified final
endclass : B

class C extends base;
constraint :initial cons2{…}
// Wrong usage: cons2 is an override from base::cons2

extern constraint :initial cons5;
// Correct usage: cons5 is not an override
endclass : C

constraint C::cons5{…}
// Correct usage: external method definitions do not declare override
specifiers
The following example uses :initial in the base class, :extends in the derived class,
and :final in the final class:
class baseCLs;
virtual function :initial void display();
$display("in base class");
…
class derCLs extends baseCLs;
virtual function :extends void display();
$display("in derived class");
…
class finalCLs extends derCLs;
function :final void display();
$display("in final class");
…
baseCLs C1 = new(); derCLs C2 = new();
finalCLs C3 = new();
C1.display();
C1 = C2; C1.display();
C1 = C3; C1.display();
end
For the following example, Base value is 5, Child value is 15, and GrandChild value is
22.
class base_class;
rand int value;

Support for initial, extends, and final Specifiers in Constraints
constraint :initial common { value inside {[1:10]}; }
function void post_randomize();
$display("Base: value=%0d",value);

class child_class extends base_class;
constraint :extends common { value inside {[11:20]}; }
function void post_randomize();
$display("Child: value=%0d",value);

class grandchild_class extends child_class;
constraint :final common { value inside {[21:30]}; }
function void post_randomize();
$display("GrandChild: value=%0d", value);
Semantic Checking
Semantic checking is implemented according to the rules in the SystemVerilog 1800-2023
LRM, section 18.5.2. The following errors are reported for wrong usage:
• Error CONS-INIT-ERROR : This error message is issued when a constraint with
:initial overrides a constraint in the base classes.
• Error CONS-EXTENDS-ERROR : This error message is issued when a constraint with
:extends does not override any constraint in the base classes.
• Error CONS-FINAL-ERROR : This error message is issued when a constraint with
:final is overridden in the derived classes.
• Error CONS-FINAL-PURE : This error message is issued when :final is applied to a
pure constraint.
The following are the example error messages:
Error-[CONS-EXTENDS-ERROR] Extends constraint not overriding any
constraint.
test.sv, 29
Extends constraint A::cons5 does not override any constriant.
A constraint with an extends specifier should override a constraint in
base
classes.

Error-[CONS-FINAL-ERROR] Final constraint overridden
test.sv, 40
Constraint B::cons2 overrides final constraint A::cons2.

A constraint with a final specifier should not be overriden in derived
classes.

Error-[CONS-INIT-ERROR] Initial constraint overriding a constraint
test.sv, 51
Initial constraint C::cons2 overrides constraint base::cons2.
