Example 212 Specifying Fresh Soft Constraints
constraint B1 {soft x == 5;}
constraint B2 {disable soft x; soft x dist {5, 8};}
B b = new();
b.randomize();
end
In Example 212, the disable soft constraint preceding the soft dist in block B2
causes the lower-priority constraint on variable x in block B1 to be discarded. Now, the
solver assigns the values 5 and 8 to x with equal distribution (the result from the fresh
constraint: soft x dist {5,8}) .
Compare the behavior of Example 212 with Example 213, where the disable soft
constraint is omitted.
Example 213 Specifying Additional Soft Constraints
constraint B1 {soft x == 5;}
constraint B3 {soft x dist {5, 8};}
B b = new();
b.randomize();
end
In Example 213, the soft dist constraint in block B3 can be satisfied with a value of 5,
so the solver assigns x the value 5. If you want the distribution weights of a soft dist
constraint to be satisfied regardless of the presence of lower-priority soft constraints, you
should first use a disable soft constraint to discard those lower-priority soft constraints.
