Support for Unconstrained Elements in Generics and Generic
Types
VCS supports unconstrained elements in the types associated with the formal generic
types in VHDL 2008 using the -vhdl08 option.
VCS supports the unconstrained elements for the formal generic types where the
arbitrarily complex derived types can be created and used via implicit subtypes, allocators,
and aggregates. You must maintain agreement between the element subtypes and the
composite subtypes in which they appear, directly or indirectly.
Example 297
package gp is
generic (type t);

type lh;
type alh is access lh;
type lt is record
l : string;
v : t;
type vlt is array (natural range <>) of lt;

type lh is record
n : alh;
v : lt;
procedure push(l : inout alh; v : t; lbl : string);
function dup(v : t; n : positive) return vlt;
end gp;
package body gp is
procedure push(l : inout alh; v : t; lbl : string) is
variable e : alh;
e := new lh'(l, (lbl, v));
l := e;
end push;

function dup(v: t; n : positive) return vlt is
constant k : lt := (to_string(n), v);
if n = 1 then
return (0 => k);
return dup(v, n - 1) & k;
end dup;
end gp;

package pt is

type bvv is array (natural range <>) of bit_vector;
type r1 is record
e1 : string;
e2 : bvv;
end pt;

use work.pt.all;

package sp is new gp generic map (r1);
The Unconstrained Elements in Generics and Generic Types feature has the following
limitations:
• The port associations at the VHDL/Verilog boundary is not supported.
• The formal sub element association and the type conversions are not supported.
• The Unconstrained Elements in Generics and Generic Types feature is not supported
for Coverage, Debug, Xprop, NLP, and Verdi.
• The array aggregates where the element type is a generic type associated with the
unconstrained elements are not supported.
Support for Connecting VHDL Records to the SV Packed Structs
You can connect VHDL records to SV packed structs by instantiating mixed design or
sva_bind to the VHDL design unit.
This feature is also applied to the following complex types:
• Arrays of arrays
• Multidimensional Arrays (MDA)
• Nested structs and records
• Packed (1-dimensional and multidimensional) arrays of structs and records
The VHDL record definition must match the SV packed struct definition, that is all the fields
of the SV packed struct must have one-to-one valid mapping to the corresponding VHDL
record members. These connections apply only to the input and output ports.

• User Interface
• Examples
User Interface
To support using packed structs across language boundaries, add the following setting to
your synopsys_sim.setup file:
---------------------------------------------------------------
-- Elaboration Settings
---------------------------------------------------------------
-- Relaxes the use of SystemVerilog arrays and structs on the
-- language boundary
MX_RELAX_ARRAYS_STRUCTS = TRUE
This also enables the following setting that handles the packed arrays on the mixed-
language design boundary:
---------------------------------------------------------------
-- Elaboration Settings
---------------------------------------------------------------
-- Relaxes the use of SystemVerilog packed arrays on the
-- language boundary
MX_RELAX_PACKED_ARRAYS = TRUE
This sections consists of the following topics:
• SystemVerilog Packed Structs
• VHDL records
• Arrays of Records
• Mapping Verilog or VHDL types
SystemVerilog Packed Structs
The packed structs in SystemVerilog can only contain packed data types and integer
types. The packed data types are the single bit data types (bit, logic, reg), packed arrays
of the single bit data types, enumerated types, and recursively other packed arrays and
packed structures.
A packed array refers to the dimensions mentioned before the variable or the object name.
• reg [5:0] array;
• bit [2:0][3:0] array;

An unpacked array refers to the dimensions mentioned after the variable or the object
name. For example,
• reg arr [3:0];
• int arr [2:0][3:0];
An unpacked array or an array with packed and unpacked dimensions is not allowed as
the packed struct member.
The 4-state data type, time, is not supported for this solution; however, it is
allowed in a packed structure.
The underlying data type of an enumeration type is int or an anonymous int as specified in
the following example:
enum {red, yellow, green} light; // anonymous int type
enum integer {IDLE, XX='x, S1='b01, S2 ='b10} state; // integer
enumeration type
The integer types with predefined widths, such as byte, shortint, int, longint, and
integer, must not have packed array dimensions declared.
The following is an example of a complex packed struct:
bit [4:0] elem1; // 2-state 1-dimensional packed array
logic [3:0][4:0] elem2; // 4-state, 2-dimensional packed array
shortint elem3; // 2-state
myenumtype elem4;
mystructtype_2 [1:0] elem5; // packed array of packed structs
} mystructtype_1;
with
typedef enum { red, yellow, green} myenumtype;
and
logic elem1;
} mystructtype_2;
You cannot define the dimensions of the struct array elements by parameters.
SystemVerilog supports the concept of packed unions, however, VHDL does
not support this concept, so the mapping is not possible.

VHDL records
The VHDL records can contain the following valid VHDL datatypes:
• 2-state and 4-state bit types, such as bit, bit-vector, std_logic,
std_logic_vector
• Integer types
• Enumeration types
• Array types. These can be 1-dimensional arrays, but also N-dimensional arrays.
• Records (nested records)
In VHDL, N-dimensional arrays can be defined as follows:
• Multidimensional arrays, such as
◦ type my_array is array(0 to 1, 3 downto 0) of std_logic
• Array-of-array, such as
◦ type my_array1 is array(7 downto 0) of bit;
◦ type my_array2 is array(1 downto 0) of my_array1;
The following is an example of a complex VHDL record:
Type my_record_type is record
Member1 : bit;
Member2 : std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
Member3 : my_array_type_1;
Member4: my_record_type_1;
Member5: my_enum_type_1;
End record my_record_type;
with
type my_array_type_1 is array (3 downto 0) of std_logic_vector(7 downto
0);
type my_record_type_1 is record member1: std_ulogic; end record
my_record_type_1;
type my_enum_type_1 is (green, yellow, red, white);
All the data types of the VHDL record members are constrained and cannot be
defined by generics.

Arrays of Records
The port types can be packed arrays of records or structs. The arrays can have multiple
dimensions which can be defined by parameters or generics, as shown in the following
type record_type is record
member1 : std_logic_vector(1 downto 0);
type record_array is array (integer range <>) of record_type;
and
logic [1:0] elem1;
} struct_type;
Instantiated in the following way:
module vlog_top();
parameter integer P1 = 1;
struct_type [0:P1] struct_var;
Design1 #(P1) design1(struct_var);
Arrays with unpacked dimensions and arrays with packed and unpacked
dimensions of the packed structs are not supported.
Mapping Verilog or VHDL types
The mapping of the struct or record port types are defined by the default mappings of each
struct member combined with a mapping of the complete struct.
You must create and maintain the struct member with the following conditions:
• The data types of each struct member must be compatible. For example, a Verilog 4-
state shortint can be mapped upon a VHDL std_logic_vector(15 down to 0) type, and
not upon a VHDL integer type.
• The number of bits for the representation of each struct member must match with that
of the other struct member that includes the array dimensions and the number of array
dimensions.
• The number, order, and types of the struct members must match.
