vlog_type_2 elem_1; // enum (32-bit integer)
vlog_type_4 elem_2; // packed array 2-dims of bit (2-state)
vlog_type_5 elem_3; // 2-state 8-bit
} vlog_type_6;

These types can be used as port types for the Mixed Language Design connections.
To use these types for the Mixed Language Design connections, the setting in
synopsys_sim.setup must be applied at the analysis and elaboration phases.
Verilog-on-top Scenario
Instantiate the following VHDL entity in Verilog using the default flow:
entity vhdl_dut is
port ( p1 : in vhdl_type_6;
p2 : out vhdl_type_6 );
end entity vhdl_dut;

module vlog_top;
vlog_type_6 sig1;
vlog_type_6 sig2;

vhdl_dut H(sig1, sig2);

This can be analyzed and elaborated according to the following default 3-step flow:
vhdlan vhdl.vhd
vlogan vlog.v
vcs vlog_top
VHDL-on-top Scenario
The same types can be used for a VHDL-on-top/Verilog-down scenario. For all scenarios,
instantiate the following Verilog module:
module vlog_dut(p1, p2);
input vlog_type_6 p1;
output vlog_type_6 p2;

Component Instantiation
Consider the following example with the component instantiation where a declaration is
made of the Verilog module that is to be instantiated:
entity vhdl_top is
end entity vhdl_top;

architecture vhdl_top_arch is
signal sig1 : vhdl_type_6;
signal sig2 : vhdl_type_6;

component vlog_dut is
port ( p1 : in vhdl_type_6;
p2 : out vhdl_type_6 );
end component vlog_dut;
V : vlog_dut port map (p1 => sig1, p2 => sig2);

end architecture vhdl_top_arch;
The actual bindings get resolved during elaboration.
Direct Instantiation
In direct instantiation, the Verilog module is not declared beforehand, however it is taken
directly from the work directory. This implies that the Verilog module is analyzed before the
VHDL entity is analyzed:
entity vhdl_top is
end entity vhdl_top;

architecture vhdl_top_arch is
signal sig1 : vhdl_type_6;
signal sig2 : vhdl_type_6;

V : entity work.vlog_dut port map (p1 => sig1, p2 => sig2);

end architecture vhdl_top_arch;
Instantiation by Means of sva_bind
With the SVA bind construct, a Verilog module can be instantiated within a VHDL scope
without changing the VHDL code. This requires the ‘bind’ instruction inside a Verilog file,
and the bind option for the elaboration.
The following example has bind instruction inside a Verilog file:
module vlog_bind;
bind vhdl_top vlog_dut Vbind(.p1(sig1), .p2(sig2));
The Verilog file is normally analyzed with vlogan, and the complete design is elaborated
with the following option to enable the sva_bind feature:
vcs vhdl_top -sva_bind_enable vlog_bind

The Connecting VHDL Records to the SV Packed Structs feature has the following
limitations:
• In SystemVerilog, all types are packed types and a packed struct in SystemVerilog can
only contain other packed members, integer types, and single bit types.
• In SystemVerilog packed structs, the concept of packed unions is not supported in
VHDL which results in no mapping.
• In the array of records, arrays with unpacked dimensions and/or arrays with packed
and unpacked dimensions of the packed structs are not supported.
• The vhdlan analysis option, -gen_sv_pkg, generates a SystemVerilog package
according to the VHDL package definition. However, it does not generate a
SystemVerilog package for the packed structs and the packed arrays. Therefore, the
generated packages cannot be used for the Mixed Language Design connections.
• Connections with the packed structs to the VHDL records are not supported for the
INOUT ports.
Mapping a SystemVerilog (Nested) Packed Struct to a VHDL
Vector Type
SystemVerliog packed structures can be mapped to the VHDL vector types and vice
versa, only if both the sides contain the same number of bits.
The supported SystemVerilog packed structures are as follows:
• Scalar and 1-D vector fields
• Packed structures and nested packed structures
• Packed array of packed structures
• Packed arrays and multidimensional packed arrays
The supported VHDL vector types are as follows:
• bit_vector: a 2-state vector type.
• std_logic_vector: a 9-state vector type, that matches to the Verilog 4-state logic
type.

To enable the mapping a SystemVerilog (nested) packed struct to a VHDL vector type
feature,
• Use the following commands:
vlogan -sverilog <file_name>
vhdlan -vhdl08 <file_name>
vcs <top_name> -R
• Set the MX_RELAX_ARRAYS_STRUCTS variable to TRUE in the synopsys_sim.setup file.
Consider the following example demonstrating the mapping of a SystemVerilog (nested)
packed struct to a VHDL vector type feature:
Test.v
logic [3:0] elem_1;
bit [1:0] elem_2;
logic elem_3;
} str;
module vlog_leaf(input str data_in, output str data_out);
assign data_out = data_in;
initial $monitor("data_out = %0b", data_out);

Top.vhd
architecture behav of top is
subtype vhdl_vector is std_logic_vector(6 downto 0);
signal sig1, sig2 : vhdl_vector;
component vlog_leaf is
port ( data_in : in vhdl_vector;
data_out : out vhdl_vector);
end component vlog_leaf;
V : vlog_leaf port map (data_in => sig1, data_out => sig2);
sig1 <= "1010101";

Run the test cases with the following commands:
vlogan -sverilog Test.v
vhdlan -vhdl08 Top.vhd
vcs top -R
data_out = 1010101
The following are the limitations of mapping the SystemVerilog (nested) packed struct to
VHDL vector type feature:
• The inout ports of the packed struct are not supported.
• The Verilog packed structs with an enumeration typed member and the packed union
members are not supported.
VHDL Unconstrained Generic Formal Sub Element Association
VCS supports the VHDL unconstrained generic Formal Sub Element Association (FSA).
Example 298 Legacy Unconstrained Array
generic(g : bit_vector);
end e;

architecture a of top is
u1 : entity work.e generic map (g(0) => '1', g(1) => '0');
Example 299 VHDL 2008 Unconstrained Element Type
package p is
type r is record
f1 : bit_vector;
f2 : string;
end p;

use work.p.all;

generic(g : r);
end e;

architecture a of top is
u1 : entity work.e generic map (g.f2 => "ab", g.f1 => "10");
u2 : entity work.e generic map (g.f2 => "ab", g.f1(0) => '1', g.f1(1)
=> '0');
u3 : entity work.e generic map (g.f1(0) => '1', g.f1(1) => '0', g.f2
=> "ab");
u4 : entity work.e generic map (g.f1(0) => '1', g.f2 => "ab", g.f1(1)
=> '0');
u5 : entity work.e generic map (g.f1(0) => '1', g.f2(1) => 'a',
g.f1(1) => '0', g.f2(2) => 'b');
The VHDL unconstrained generic FSA feature is not supported for multidimensional arrays
FSA.
VHDL 2008 Generics in Subprograms
VCS is enhanced to support the VHDL 2008 LRM generics in subprograms feature. For a
VHDL 2008 feature, you must use the vhdlan -vhdl08 option.
You can enable the VHDL 2008 generics in subprograms feature using one of the
following methods:
• Use the vhdlan -Xgens_in_subp option.
• Set the GENS_IN_SUBP environment variable as True in the synopsys_sim.setup file.
The following example shows how you can supply a data type in a generic map:
Example 300 VHDL 2008 Generics in Subprograms
package p is
function eq generic (type t)
parameter (x, y : t) return boolean;
end p;

package body p is
function eq generic (type t)
parameter (x, y : t) return boolean is
return x = y;
end function;
end p;

use work.p.all;

entity e is end e;

signal b : bit;
signal n : natural;
signal v : bit_vector(1 to 4) := "1011";
procedure eqg generic (type t) parameter(x, y : t) is
assert x = y;
end procedure;
You can instantiate the procedure and function to get versions for various types in the
following way:
procedure eqpb is new eqg generic map(bit);
function eqfb is new eq generic map(bit);
procedure eqpn is new eqg generic map(natural);
function eqfn is new eq generic map(natural);
procedure eqpv is new eqg generic map(bit_vector(1 to 4));
function eqfv is new eq generic map(bit_vector(1 to 4));
The eqpb and eqfb identifiers are the names of the subprogram instances; and the
generic map supplies actual generics for the formal generics defined by the uninstantiated
subprogram. If all the formal generics have default values, you can omit the generic map
implying the use of default values. After instantiating the subprogram, you can use the
instance name to call the instance.
eqpb(b, '0');
assert eqfb(b, '0');
eqpn(n, 0);
assert eqfn(n, 0);
eqpv(v, "1011");
assert eqfv(v, "1011");
The VHDL 2008 Generics in Subprograms feature is not supported in the KDB, Coverage,
and Xprop flows.

VHDL 2008 Record Type Conversions
With VHDL 2008, you can declare records with the elements of the unbounded array
types, which can be constrained later when declaring objects, interfaces, subtypes, and
more. This feature ensures that the record type conversions validate the bounds of the
operand against the target subtype.
The subtype validations applied to the record type conversions must be the same as the
ones applied in assignments, subprogram calls, and so on. The VHDL 2008 Record Type
Conversions feature supports conversions to fully constrained record type or subtype.
To enable the VHDL 2008 record type conversions feature, use the -vhdl08 compile
option with vhdlan, as follows:
vhdlan –vhdl08 <file_name>
The following example demonstrates the use of unconstrained and constrained types in
VHDL, specifically focusing on records, arrays, and subtypes:
package p is
type bvv is array (natural range <>) of bit_vector;
type mdab is array (natural range <>, natural range <>) of bit;
type ur is record
e0 : bit_vector;
e1 : bvv;
e2 : mdab;

end p;

package body p is

end p;

use work.p.all;

entity leaf is
generic (g : ur);
end leaf;

architecture a of leaf is
constant k1 : ur := g;

use work.p.all;

architecture a of top is
component leaf
generic (g : ur);

constant k1 : ur := (X"AA", (X"A", X"B", X"C", X"D"),
(X"0", X"1", X"2", X"3"));
subtype t1 is k1'subtype;

-- fully constrained, different bounds, same sizes
subtype tfc is ur (e0 (107 downto 100),
e1 (104 downto 101)(50 to 53),
e2 (53 downto 50, 33 downto 30));

t : process

i1 : leaf generic map (k1);
i2 : leaf generic map (tfc(k1));
The VHDL 2008 record type conversions feature has the following limitations:
• The partially constrained array subtypes are not supported.
• Type conversions to a record subtype with unconstrained elements are not supported.
VHDL 2008 Use Clauses
VHDL 2008 supports the use clauses for type declarations feature. This feature ensures
that all the predefined operations and any overloaded declarations of the predefined
operations in the same scope are visible.
The following is the supported list of features:
Predefined operations
• Logical operations: and or nand nor xor xnor (binary and unary)
• Comparison: =, /=, <, <=, >, >=, ?=, ?/=, ?<, ?<=, ?>, ?>=, ??
• Shift and rotate: sll, srl, sla, sra, rol, ror
• Arithmetic operations: + - (binary and unary), &, * / mod rem , ** abs not

• Reduction: minimum, maximum
• String conversion: to_string
• File operations: file_open, file_close, read, write, endfile, read, write
(unconstrained), file_open (with status)
• deallocate
On declarations
• Types and subtypes
◦ Scalar (enumeration (character type), integer, floating-point, physical)
◦ Composite (Array, Record)
◦ Access
◦ File
• Aliases of all the types and sub types
To enable the VHDL 2008 use clauses feature,
• Use the -vhdl08 compile option with vhdlan, as follows:
vhdlan –vhdl08 <file_name>
• Set the VHDL2008_USE_CLAUSE variable to TRUE in the synopsys_sim.setup file.
The following example demonstrates the use of custom types, overloaded operators, and
assertions in VHDL:
package pack is
type color_type is (red, orange, yellow, green, blue, violet);
function "<" ( c1, c2 : color_type ) return boolean;
type color_vector is array (natural range <>) of color_type;
type bv is array (natural range <>) of bit;
end stuff;

architecture a of top is

proc_1 : process
use work.pack.color_type;

