SystemVerilog Packed Structs
The packed structs in SystemVerilog can only contain packed data types and integer
types. The packed data types are the single bit data types (bit, logic, reg), packed arrays
of the single bit data types, enumerated types, and recursively other packed arrays and
packed structures.
A packed array refers to the dimensions mentioned before the variable or the object name.
• reg [5:0] array;
• bit [2:0][3:0] array;

An unpacked array refers to the dimensions mentioned after the variable or the object
name. For example,
• reg arr [3:0];
• int arr [2:0][3:0];
An unpacked array or an array with packed and unpacked dimensions is not allowed as
the packed struct member.
The 4-state data type, time, is not supported for this solution; however, it is
allowed in a packed structure.
The underlying data type of an enumeration type is int or an anonymous int as specified in
the following example:
enum {red, yellow, green} light; // anonymous int type
enum integer {IDLE, XX='x, S1='b01, S2 ='b10} state; // integer
enumeration type
The integer types with predefined widths, such as byte, shortint, int, longint, and
integer, must not have packed array dimensions declared.
The following is an example of a complex packed struct:
bit [4:0] elem1; // 2-state 1-dimensional packed array
logic [3:0][4:0] elem2; // 4-state, 2-dimensional packed array
shortint elem3; // 2-state
myenumtype elem4;
mystructtype_2 [1:0] elem5; // packed array of packed structs
} mystructtype_1;
with
typedef enum { red, yellow, green} myenumtype;
and
logic elem1;
} mystructtype_2;
You cannot define the dimensions of the struct array elements by parameters.
SystemVerilog supports the concept of packed unions, however, VHDL does

type vhdl_type_6 is record
Member_0 : vhdl_type_1; -- 4-state vector
Member_1 : vhdl_type_2; -- enum (32-bit integer)
Member_2 : vhdl_type_4; MDA of bit 2-state
Member_3 : vhdl_type_5; -- 2-state vector
end record vhdl_type_6;
maps to
typedef logic [3:0] vlog_type_1;
typedef enum {green, yellow, red, blue, purple, black, white}
vlog_type_2;
typedef bit vlog_type_3;
typedef vlog_type_3 [1:0][2:0] vlog_type_4;
typedef byte vlog_type_5;

vlog_type_1 elem_0; // 4-state vector
vlog_type_2 elem_1; // enum (32-bit integer)
vlog_type_4 elem_2; // packed array 2-dims of bit (2-state)
vlog_type_5 elem_3; // 2-state 8-bit
} vlog_type_6;

These types can be used as port types for the Mixed Language Design connections.
To use these types for the Mixed Language Design connections, the setting in
synopsys_sim.setup must be applied at the analysis and elaboration phases.
Verilog-on-top Scenario
Instantiate the following VHDL entity in Verilog using the default flow:
entity vhdl_dut is
port ( p1 : in vhdl_type_6;
p2 : out vhdl_type_6 );
end entity vhdl_dut;

module vlog_top;
vlog_type_6 sig1;
vlog_type_6 sig2;

vhdl_dut H(sig1, sig2);

This can be analyzed and elaborated according to the following default 3-step flow:
vhdlan vhdl.vhd
vlogan vlog.v
vcs vlog_top
VHDL-on-top Scenario
The same types can be used for a VHDL-on-top/Verilog-down scenario. For all scenarios,
instantiate the following Verilog module:
module vlog_dut(p1, p2);
input vlog_type_6 p1;
output vlog_type_6 p2;

