VHDL 2008 Generics in Subprograms
VCS is enhanced to support the VHDL 2008 LRM generics in subprograms feature. For a
VHDL 2008 feature, you must use the vhdlan -vhdl08 option.
You can enable the VHDL 2008 generics in subprograms feature using one of the
following methods:
• Use the vhdlan -Xgens_in_subp option.
• Set the GENS_IN_SUBP environment variable as True in the synopsys_sim.setup file.
The following example shows how you can supply a data type in a generic map:
Example 300 VHDL 2008 Generics in Subprograms
package p is
function eq generic (type t)
parameter (x, y : t) return boolean;
end p;

package body p is
function eq generic (type t)
parameter (x, y : t) return boolean is
return x = y;
end function;
end p;

use work.p.all;

entity e is end e;

signal b : bit;
signal n : natural;
signal v : bit_vector(1 to 4) := "1011";
procedure eqg generic (type t) parameter(x, y : t) is
assert x = y;
end procedure;
You can instantiate the procedure and function to get versions for various types in the
following way:
procedure eqpb is new eqg generic map(bit);
function eqfb is new eq generic map(bit);
procedure eqpn is new eqg generic map(natural);
function eqfn is new eq generic map(natural);
procedure eqpv is new eqg generic map(bit_vector(1 to 4));
function eqfv is new eq generic map(bit_vector(1 to 4));
The eqpb and eqfb identifiers are the names of the subprogram instances; and the
generic map supplies actual generics for the formal generics defined by the uninstantiated
subprogram. If all the formal generics have default values, you can omit the generic map
implying the use of default values. After instantiating the subprogram, you can use the
instance name to call the instance.
eqpb(b, '0');
assert eqfb(b, '0');
eqpn(n, 0);
assert eqfn(n, 0);
eqpv(v, "1011");
assert eqfv(v, "1011");
The VHDL 2008 Generics in Subprograms feature is not supported in the KDB, Coverage,
and Xprop flows.

VHDL 2008 Record Type Conversions
With VHDL 2008, you can declare records with the elements of the unbounded array
types, which can be constrained later when declaring objects, interfaces, subtypes, and
more. This feature ensures that the record type conversions validate the bounds of the
operand against the target subtype.
The subtype validations applied to the record type conversions must be the same as the
ones applied in assignments, subprogram calls, and so on. The VHDL 2008 Record Type
Conversions feature supports conversions to fully constrained record type or subtype.
To enable the VHDL 2008 record type conversions feature, use the -vhdl08 compile
option with vhdlan, as follows:
vhdlan –vhdl08 <file_name>
The following example demonstrates the use of unconstrained and constrained types in
VHDL, specifically focusing on records, arrays, and subtypes:
package p is
type bvv is array (natural range <>) of bit_vector;
type mdab is array (natural range <>, natural range <>) of bit;
type ur is record
e0 : bit_vector;
e1 : bvv;
e2 : mdab;

end p;

package body p is

end p;

use work.p.all;

entity leaf is
generic (g : ur);
end leaf;

architecture a of leaf is
constant k1 : ur := g;

use work.p.all;

architecture a of top is
component leaf
generic (g : ur);

constant k1 : ur := (X"AA", (X"A", X"B", X"C", X"D"),
(X"0", X"1", X"2", X"3"));
subtype t1 is k1'subtype;

-- fully constrained, different bounds, same sizes
subtype tfc is ur (e0 (107 downto 100),
e1 (104 downto 101)(50 to 53),
e2 (53 downto 50, 33 downto 30));

t : process

i1 : leaf generic map (k1);
i2 : leaf generic map (tfc(k1));
The VHDL 2008 record type conversions feature has the following limitations:
• The partially constrained array subtypes are not supported.
• Type conversions to a record subtype with unconstrained elements are not supported.
VHDL 2008 Use Clauses
VHDL 2008 supports the use clauses for type declarations feature. This feature ensures
that all the predefined operations and any overloaded declarations of the predefined
operations in the same scope are visible.
The following is the supported list of features:
Predefined operations
• Logical operations: and or nand nor xor xnor (binary and unary)
• Comparison: =, /=, <, <=, >, >=, ?=, ?/=, ?<, ?<=, ?>, ?>=, ??
• Shift and rotate: sll, srl, sla, sra, rol, ror
• Arithmetic operations: + - (binary and unary), &, * / mod rem , ** abs not

• Reduction: minimum, maximum
• String conversion: to_string
• File operations: file_open, file_close, read, write, endfile, read, write
(unconstrained), file_open (with status)
• deallocate
On declarations
• Types and subtypes
◦ Scalar (enumeration (character type), integer, floating-point, physical)
◦ Composite (Array, Record)
◦ Access
◦ File
• Aliases of all the types and sub types
To enable the VHDL 2008 use clauses feature,
• Use the -vhdl08 compile option with vhdlan, as follows:
vhdlan –vhdl08 <file_name>
• Set the VHDL2008_USE_CLAUSE variable to TRUE in the synopsys_sim.setup file.
