Type-coercion and converter-insertion features are enhanced to improve the quality.
This section describes the various type-coercion and converter-insertion enhancements
available in VCS:
Enhancements for Type-Coercion
The following section describes the enhancements for type-coercion:
• Support for Basic Type-Coercion
• Enhancement to Type Coercion Issues for SV Interface
• Support for Coercion With Tran Gate
• Support for Four-State Control Signal
• Support for Units #1n, #1u, and #1p
Support for Basic Type-Coercion
The basic type-coercion feature allows concrete types to propagate bottom-up only. You
can enable basic type-coercion using the -coerce=basic option.

Figure 183 Basic Type-Coercion
Enhancement to Type-Coercion Issues for SV Interface
VCS supports the following SystemVerilog (SV) interface scenarios:
• One or more wires/interconnects of SV interface are coerced due to type-coercion.
• The wires/interconnects of this SV interface are coerced differently in different
instances of this interface.
• A SV interface that has one or more virtual interfaces.
Support for Coercion With Tran Gate
Type-coercion through tran gates is supported under the option -adopt
coerce_thru_tran.
For example, if an interconnect is connected to one bidirectional pin of a tran switch,
where the other bidirectional pin of that tran is connected to a System-Verilog nettype
wrealsum, then under the option -adopt coerce_thru_tran , the interconnect gets
coerced to wreralsum.
The following are the examples for coercion using the switch -adopt coerce_thru_tran :
Example 1: A local interconnect x gets coerced to wrealsum
wrealsum w1;

interconnect x;
tran t1(w1,x);
Example 2: A wire-interconnect in an interface is connected to tran through XMR, and gets
coerced to nettype wrealsum
interface ifc();
wire itc;

ifc if1();
wrealsum gpio_ana_pad;

ana_cell_top i_ana_cell_top (
.out_en (1'b0),
.out (1'b0),
.in (),
.ana (),
.control (1'b0),
.pad (gpio_ana_pad)
);
tranif0 (if1.itc,i_ana_cell_top.pad,1'b0);
Local signals and XMR signals are coerced through tran gates.
A wreal construct supported in SystemVerilog with the -realport option is not supported
for a coercion through transmission gates. VCS supports automatic conversion of a wreal
construct into equivalent SystemVerilog user-defined Nettypes. For more information, see
the Support for Converting Wreal to Nettype.
Support for Four-State Control Signal
The four-state control signal is supported by the -xlrm ams_4state_tgate_control
compile-time option.
Consider the following example where two net segments of type wrealsum are connected
to the two bidirectional pins of a tranif0 gate, and the control pin of this tranif0 gate is
connected to a four-state signal of type wire.
The test case and its outputs are as follows:
wrealsum a, b;
wire ctrl;
reg [0:3]val = {1'b1, 1'b0, 1'bx, 1'bz};
real val_real[0:1] = {1.1, 2.1};

