onvert real to
integer and integer to real. An uvm_report_object handle can be made available, which
is useful for issuing unified messages.
Example 33 sv_ams_real Constructor
function sv_ams_real::new(real r=0.0,
real acc=`SV_AMS_REAL_DEF_ACCURACY,
string name = "");
An important aspect of UVM-AMS is the possibility to randomize real values.
Though this capability is not available in SystemVerilog, it is made possible with the
sv_ams_real::urandom() method.
As shown in Example 34, a sv_ams_real instance is constructed with a default of 0.0 and
randomized with boundaries comprised between 0 to 1.8. By using this technique, the
sv_ams_real instance can be used to deposit a voltage value to an analog node, which
can take random values from 0.0 to 1.8V.

UVM Real Support
You are using a default distribution that is similar to white noise injection.
Example 34 Randomizing sv_ams_real Instance
program automatic test;
sv_ams_real r = new(0.0);

int i;
for(i=0; i<100; i++) begin
r.urandom(0.0,1.8);
$display("i=%0d - r=%f", i, r.get_real());
end
end
endprogram
Another important aspect of UVM-AMS is the possibility to automatically compare real
values with a predefined tolerance. This is necessary for modeling reference models or
to wait for an analog node to reach a given value. This capability is available with the
sv_ams_real::cmp(a,b,tol) method.
As shown in Example 35, the sv_ams_real method can be used to wait for a particular
analog node to reach a defined real value. In this example, the task check_frequency
measures the frequency of the v analog node, which is in the aif.sck clocking block
interface. This task waits for v to reach vmax, then vmin and measures t0. Similarly, it
waits for v to reach again vmax, then vmin and measure t1. Therefore, the frequency can
simply be calculated by subtracting t0 to t1.
Example 35 Waiting for a sv_ams_real Value
task check_frequency(real frequency, tolerance=0.01);
real t0,t1,f;

wait(sv_ams_real::cmp(aif.sck.v,vmax,tolerance));
wait(sv_ams_real::cmp(aif.sck.v,vmin,tolerance));
t0=$realtime;

wait(sv_ams_real::cmp(aif.sck.v,vmax,tolerance));
wait(sv_ams_real::cmp(aif.sck.v,vmin,tolerance));
t1=$realtime;

f=1/(t1-t0);

In conjunction with Example 35, Example 36 shows how to use sv_ams_real::cmp
method to compare the measured frequency against the expected one with a predefined
tolerance of +/- 1%.

UVM-AMS Mathematical Functions
Example 36 Comparing to a sv_ams_real Value

if (!(sv_ams_real::cmp(f,frequency,tolerance)))
'uvm_error(this.log,
$psprintf("Expecting f=%f, %f with tol=%0.3f%%",
frequency, f, (tolerance*100)));
