Virtual registers are always arrays because the usual reason they are virtual is that there
are a large number of them and implementing them in a RAM instead of individual flip-
flops is most efficient. Arrays of virtual registers are associated with a memory. The
association of a virtual register array with a memory can be static (for example, specified
in the RALF file) or dynamic (for example, specified at runtime through user code).
Static virtual registers are associated with a specific memory and are located at specific
offsets within that memory. The association is specified in the RALF file and is created by
the code generator. This association is permanent and cannot be broken at runtime.
Example 15 Static virtual register array
block MAC {

memory DMABFRS { }

virtual register CHANNEL[1024] DMABFRS@0 {
field {};

