Back-door Access In VMM RAL
Back-door access lets you verify registers and memory locations efficiently. Use back-door
access to find errors that standard read and write cycles might miss. For example, if you
access the wrong memory or reverse data bits, a bug introduced during a write can be
undone during a read.
Back-door access improves verification speed. Access registers and memory locations
with minimal simulation time. After you confirm the physical interface works, use back-
door access to remove simulation time for configuring the device under test (DUT). This
process can save significant time.
Back-door access works by directly reaching simulation constructs that represent
registers or memory models. Use the hierarchical name in the design structure. The main
challenges are finding and maintaining the correct hierarchical path and understanding the
simulation constructs used.
The only limits on back-door access are the features of your simulation language and
environment.
Back-door Read/Write Versus Peek/Poke
You can perform back-door access to registers and memory by using the following read/
write methods:
• “ vmm_ral_field::read()”
• “ vmm_ral_field::write()”
• “ vmm_ral_mem::read()”
• “ vmm_ral_mem::write()”
• “ vmm_ral_reg::read()”
• “ vmm_ral_reg::write()”
Or the following peek/poke methods:
• “ vmm_ral_field::peek()”
• “ vmm_ral_field::poke()”

• “ vmm_ral_mem::peek()”
• “ vmm_ral_mem::poke()”
• “ vmm_ral_reg::peek()”
• “ vmm_ral_reg::poke()”
The peek() methods return the actual value read using the back-back-door without
modifying the content of the register or memory. Should the register content be modified
upon a normal read operation, such as a clear-on-read field, it will not be modified.
Therefore, reading using peek() methods may yield different results than reading through
read() methods.
The poke() methods deposit the specified value directly in the register or memory. Should
the register contain non-writable bits or bits that do not reflect the exact value written, such
as a read-update or write-1-to-clear fields, they will contain a different value than if the
same value had been written through normal means. All field values, regardless of their
access mode, will be forced to the poked value. Therefore, writing using poke() methods
may yield different results than writing through the frontdoor.
Some design implementations may not allow values to be poked. For example,
a read-only register that is implemented by sampling signal values or constants
cannot be poked. In such instances, a vmm_rw::ERROR status should be
returned.
When using the read() methods with a back-door access path, the behavior of the
register or memory access mimics the same access performed using a front-door access.
For example, reading a register containing a clear-on-read field will cause the field value to
be cleared by poking 0’s into it.
When using the write() method with a back-door access path, the behavior of the
register or memory access mimics the same access performed using a front-door access.
For example, writing to a read-only field using back-door access will cause the field value
to be maintained by first peeking its current value then poking it back in instead of the
specified value (unless the entire register is composed of read-only fields, in which case,
the entire write operation is ignored).
Automatically generated back-door mechanisms are associated with their corresponding
register or memory abstraction class when the RAL model containing these registers
and memories is instantiated. However, to enable the automatic generation of back-
door access, it is necessary to specify the hierarchical path to the HDL structures that

implement the register or memory. This is accomplished by using the hdl_path attributes in
field, register, regfile, memory, block, and system instantiations of the RALF specification.
The generated back-door simply concatenates the path elements specified in the
individual hdl_path attributes to form the complete path to the target register or
memory. For example, the RALF file shown in Example 51 would yield the path
S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i.dec.r1_reg to the register r1 .
Example 51 RALF Description with hdl_path Specifications
system s1 {

block b1 (b1_i) @'h1000 {

register r1 dec.r1_reg {

}
}
}
For a path to be well-formed, a RALF regfile, block, or system must correspond
to a design module or entity instance. For example, the (partial) RTL code shown
in Example 52 represents the structure of the design matching the specification in
Example 51.
Example 52 RTL Structure
module b1();

always @ (posedge clk)
begin: dec
reg [7:0] r1_reg;
if (rst) r1_reg <= 0;
else if () r1_reg <= ;
end

module s1();

b1 b1_i();

module tb_top;

s1 dut();

The absolute path to the instance of the DUT that corresponds to the RAL model is
specified by defining the name_TOP_PATH symbol where name is the uppercase name of

the top-level block or system in the RAL model. Using the structure shown in Example 52,
the S1_TOP_PATH symbol must be defined to tb_top.dut, as shown in the following:
% vcs +define+S1_TOP_PATH=tb_top.dut \
ral_s1.sv
Arrays
If the RALF specification contains arrays of system, block, regfile, register, or field
instances (see “Arrays and Register Files” for more details on arrays of instances), the
hdl_path attribute must contain a %d, [%d] or [%g] format specifier.
Example 53 shows the key differences between them.
Example 53 A RALF Description Using Different Types of Array Back-door
system s1 {
block b1[2] (b1_i%d) {
register r1 (dec.r1_reg) {
field f
}
}
block b2 (blk2) {
register r2[2] (r2_array[%d]) {
field f
}
}
block b3[2] (b3_gen_array[%g].blk) {
register r3 (dec.r3_reg) {
field f
}
}
}
This section consists of the following topics:
• %d Format Specifier
• [%d] Format Specifier
• [%g] Format Specifier
%d Format Specifier
You should use the %d format specifier when the corresponding back-door RTL
implementation of the RALF array is not really an array, rather a series of similarly named
non-array signals, for example, block b1 array in Example 53.

Example 54 shows that the generated back-door path of such an RALF array does not
have any array in it.
Example 54 Generated Code During the %d Format Specifier Usage
class ral_reg_s1_b1_r1_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
int b1;

function new(int b1);
this.b1 = b1;

output bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
case (b1)
0: data = `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i0.dec.r1_reg;
1: data = `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i1.dec.r1_reg;

input bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
case (b1)
0: `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i0.dec.r1_reg = data;
1: `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i1.dec.r1_reg = data;
An example of the RTL implementation of a RALF array, which is not an array in the back-
door/RTL, rather a series of similarly named non-array signals is as follows:
module s1();

b1 b1_i0();
b1 b1_i1();

[%d] Format Specifier
You should use the [%d] format specifier only when the end signal/variable of the
corresponding back-door RTL path/implementation is actually an array, but not a
generated instance array. For example, block r2 as shown in Example 53.

The advantage of having a normal array instead of generated instance array is that you
can access an each array element by indexing with a variable as shown in Example 55.
Example 55 Generated Code During the [%d] Format Specifier Usage
class ral_reg_s1_b2_r2_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
int r2;

function new(int r2);
this.r2 = r2;

output bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
data = `S1_TOP_PATH.blk2.r2_array[r2];

input bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
`S1_TOP_PATH.blk2.r2_array[r2] = data;
[%g] Format Specifier
Use the [%g] format specifier when the corresponding back-door RTL implementation of
the RALF array is a generated instance array. For example, block b3 array as shown in
Example 56.
Because the generated instance array cannot be indexed using any variable,
numeric constants are used for indexing them.
Example 56 Generated Code During the [%g] Format specifier Usage
class ral_reg_s1_b3_r3_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
int b3;

function new(int b3);
this.b3 = b3;

output bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,

case (b3)
0: data = `S1_TOP_PATH.b3_gen_array[0].blk.dec.r3_reg;
1: data = `S1_TOP_PATH.b3_gen_array[1].blk.dec.r3_reg;

input bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
case (b3)
0: `S1_TOP_PATH.b3_gen_array[0].blk.dec.r3_reg = data;
1: `S1_TOP_PATH.b3_gen_array[1].blk.dec.r3_reg = data;
You should use the [%g] format specifier when the back-door RTL path has an array
which is not the end signal/variable of that back-door RTL path. For example, block b3
array shown in Example 56.
You cannot index an XMR using variable, unless it is an end signal/variable. In
this case you use numeric constants to index them.
VHDL
Automatic back-door access generation is supported for VHDL designs, or registers/
memories located in a portion of the design where the end signal has been implemented/
described using VHDL and a new RALF keyword called, vhdl_path. You use vhdl_path
to specify back-door RTL paths ending in a VHDL signal or a variable.
Currently the VHDL variables are not supported as there is a dependency on
$hdl_xmr for the support and $hdl_xmr does not support the VHDL variables
currently.
Example 57 RAL Backdoor Support for VHDL Signals
system top {
block blk vhdl_path=(vhd_top_mod:dut_blk) {
bytes 1;
register reg vhdl_path =(reg) {
…
}

}
}
You need to consider reading Example 57 in bold as RALF syntactic keywords.
In Example 57, the RALF keyword, vhdl_path is used to specify the back-door RTL
path of the VHDL signals. Hierarchical concatenation rules of hdl_paths also apply for
vhdl_path.
For example, in above-shown RALF file, the final synthesized/concatenated back-door
access path for the register reg is vhd_top_mod:dut_blk:reg. For a given RALF
construct, you can either specify a vhdl_path or a Verilog hdl_path and not both.
If the RALF description of the top-level block/system has vhdl_path specified for
it or any of its descendents and ralgen command line options, -b and -top_path
are used, then ralgen creates a file, ral_vhdl_bkdrs_<top>.v. The ralgen
generated file, ral_vhdl_bkdrs_<top>.v has the definition of a module called,
ral_<top>_vhdl_bkdr_connector.
For each VHDL signal for which back-door access path needs to be generated
using $hdl_xmr, this module have the corresponding Verilog side reg definition (of
appropriate size) for use/mapping with $hdl_xmr and an initial block which would have the
corresponding $hdl_xmr connection commands.
For a read/write VHDL implemented register, you should create one Verilog reg definition
for reading and one for writing. These intermediate Verilog regs are named after their
corresponding synthesized/concatenated hierarchical/absolute (v)hdl_path names only
after replacing ':' or '.' with '__' and adding a '__' prefix to it. To indicate the direction of data
flow/assignment, a '__ip' or '__op' suffix is also added.
An example of such module for the RALF described is shown in the following example:
module ral_top_vhdl_bkdr_connector;
reg[7:0] __vhd_top_mod__dut_blk__reg__ip;
reg[7:0] __vhd_top_mod__dut_blk__reg__op;

$hdl_xmr("<top_path>.vhd_top_mod.dut_blk.reg",
"__vhd_top_mod__dut_blk__reg__ip");
$hdl_xmr("__vhd_top_mod__dut_blk__reg__op",
"<top_path>.vhd_top_mod.dut_blk.reg");
end
Here, the <top_path> is the absolute top-level XMR path of the VHDL design instance
which must be provided with the new ralgen command line option -top_path or -p.
For generating RAL VHDL back-door code, you should specify this option apart from -b
ralgen command line option.

Take an instance of this ralgen generated module and specify the absolute top-level XMR
path of this instance in the compile time macro <top>_VHDL_BKDR_CONNECTOR. By doing
this, the RAL back-door implementation classes can access the VHDL DUT registers/
memories through this connection module instance as shown in the following example:
class ral_reg_top_blk_reg_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
output bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
:
data = `TOP_VHDL_BKDR_CONNECTOR.__vhd_top_mod__dut_blk__reg__ip;
input bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
`TOP_VHDL_BKDR_CONNECTOR.__vhd_top_mod__dut_blk__reg__op = data;
:
:
Scoping Back-door Classes to SystemVerilog Package
The generated back-door classes with the cross-module reference based back-doors
cannot be packaged into a SystemVerilog package as the HDL XMRs are a part of the
generated classes. This feature supports packaging into SystemVerilog package using a
virtual instance to access the HDL XMRs.
Command-Line Option
-gen_vif_bkdr
When specified, helps the generated model to have a register model free of HDL XMRs in
its back-door classes.
Specification
• It is necessary to specify the -boption when specifying the -gen_vif_bkdr option.
• A SystemVerilog interface is generated (in a separate file) additional to the register
model itself which you need to pass to the compile command-line. Additional setup is
not required.
• It is necessary to specify -gen_vif_bkdr when -b is specified with -P.

Consider the following code generated without the -gen_vif_bkdr option:
class ral_reg_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
virtual task read(output vmm_rw::status_e status, output
vmm_ral_data_t data,
input int data_id, input int
scenario_id, input int stream_id);
logic [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] x_data;
super.pre_read(data_id, scenario_id, stream_id);
x_data = {
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_2.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_1.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_0.DataOut
};
data = x_data;
if($isunknown(x_data))
status = vmm_rw::HAS_X;
super.post_read(status, data, data_id, scenario_id,
stream_id);
When the -gen_vif_bkdr option is included, the same back-door class is generated as
follows:
class ral_reg_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
virtual ral_oc_ethernet_intf __reg_vif;
virtual task read(output vmm_rw::status_e status, output
vmm_ral_data_t data,
input int data_id, input int
scenario_id, input int stream_id);
logic [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] x_data;
super.pre_read(data_id, scenario_id, stream_id);
__reg_vif.ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_read(x_data);
data = x_data;
if($isunknown(x_data))
status = vmm_rw::HAS_X;
super.post_read(status, data, data_id, scenario_id,
stream_id);

virtual task write(output vmm_rw::status_e status, input
vmm_ral_data_t data,
input int data_id, input int
scenario_id, input int stream_id);
super.pre_write(data, data_id, scenario_id, stream_id);
__reg_vif.ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_write(data);

super.post_write(status, data, data_id, scenario_id,
stream_id);
The actual XMR access is placed inside the interface generated by RAL. The generated
interface is generated inside the file named ral_<top_name>_intf.sv and the generated
code is as follows:
interface ral_oc_ethernet_intf;
`include "vmm_ral.sv";
`ifdef VCS
ral_oc_ethernet_vif_container_pkg::vif_wrapper::set_vif(interface::self(
));
`endif
task ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_read(output
vmm_ral_data_t x_data);
x_data = {
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_2.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_1.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_0.DataOut
};

task ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_write(input vmm_ral_data_t
data);
{
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_2.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_1.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_0.DataOut
} = data;
endinterface
This allows you to place the RAL extended classes to be scoped to the SystemVerilog
package. You need to include the generated ral_<top_name>_intf.sv file during
compilation and instantiate the interface.
Target Structures
The automatically-generated back-door access code must make certain assumptions
about the nature of the HDL code used to implement the register and memory being
accessed. Although there are almost unlimited ways you can implement a register, there
are only a few styles that are supported by the back-door access generator. It is important
that, when implementing registers and memories in RTL code, a suitable coding style be
used.
The following guidelines outline the restrictions on RTL structures used to implement
registers and memories to enable automatic generation of their back-door access. Some

of these restrictions may be removed in the future as the capabilities of the back-door
access generator are improved.
If the target structures do not meet the requirements for automatic generation of back-
door access, a user-defined back-door access mechanism must be created, as specified
in User-Defined Back-doors in VMM RAL.
Writable Fields and memories must be implemented using "reg".
When performing a back-door write operation, a blocking procedural assignment is used.
This requires that the target of the assignment be a reg.
Read-only fields may be implemented using wire, parameter or Boolean expression.
Such structures cannot be written to, therefore, only the read back-door access to a read-
only field is generated. Attempting a back-door write to a read-only field results in an error.
Example 58 Read-only Field Implemented Using an Expression
always @ (*)
if (wr) rdat = 'Z;
else case (addr)

16'h0010: rdat = {fifo_fl, fifo_mt};

end
Example 59 RALF Description for Read-only Field
register r1 @'h0010{
field mt (fifo_mt) {
bits 1;
reset 1;
}
field fl (fifo_fl) {
bits 1;
reset 0;
}
}
Example 60 Alternative RALF Description for Read-only Field
register r1 (fifo_fl, fifo_mt) @'h0010{
field mt {
bits 1;
reset 1;
}

field fl {
bits 1;
reset 0;
}
}
A register may implement all of its fields in a single "reg".
A register may be composed of more than one field. All these different fields may be
implemented in the same reg that implements the overall register. This implies that all bits
in the register, up to the most-significant bits of the most-significant field, are implemented
and there are no reserved or unused bits between fields. In that case, no hdl_path should
be specified in field instantiations in the register specification.
For example, the register specified using the register definition shown in Example 61, can
be implemented using the RTL code shown in Example 62. The reg named r1_reg is used
to implement fields f1 and f2.
Example 61 Register with Multiple Fields
register r1 (r1_reg) @'h0010{
field f1 {
reset 4'hA;
}
bits 8;
reset 8'h55;
}
}
Example 62 Single-reg- Implementation of Register with Multiple Fields
reg [11:0] r1_reg;
always @ (posedge clk)
if (rst) r1_reg <= {8'h55, 4'hA};
else if (wr) case (addr)

16'h0010: r1_reg <= wdat;

end

always @ (*)
if (wr) rdat = 'Z;
else case (addr)

16'h0010: rdat = r1_reg;

end
If per-field peek()/poke() operations are required (not yet supported), each field instance
should have its respective bit slice specified in its hdl_pathattribute. For example,
the register specified using the register definition shown in Example 63, can also be
implemented using the RTL code shown in Example 62.
Example 63 Register with Multiple Fields
register r1 @'h0010{
field f1 (r1_reg[3:0]) {
reset 4'hA;
}
field f2 (r1_reg[11:4]) {
bits 8;
reset 8'h55;
}
}
A register may implement its fields in separate "reg".
A register may be composed of more than one field. All these different fields may
be implemented in different regs that each implement one field. The register is the
concatenation of these individual regs. This implementation allows reserved or unused bits
between fields. In that case, the hdl_path must be specified in field instantiations in the
register specification.
For example, the register specified using the register definition shown in Example 64, can
be implemented using the RTL code shown in Example 65. The regs named f1_reg and
f2_reg are used to implement fields f1 and f2 respectively. Additionally, both Example 58
and Example 59 show an example of a register implemented using separate constructs for
separate read-only fields.
Example 64 Register with Multiple Fields
register r1 @'h0010{
field f1 (f1_reg) {
reset 4'hA;
}
field f2 (f2_reg) @8 {
reset 4'h5;
}
}

Example 65 Multiple-reg- Implementation of Register with Multiple Fields
reg [3:0] f1_reg, f2_reg;
always @ (posedge clk)
if (rst) begin
f1_reg <= 4'hA;
f2_reg <= 4'h5};
end
else if (wr) case (addr)

16'h0010: begin
f1_reg <= wdat[3:0];
f2_reg <= wdat[11:8];
end

end

always @ (*)
if (wr) rdat = 'Z;
else case (addr)

16'h0010: rdat = {f2_reg, 4'h0, f1_reg};

end
A field may be implemented using multiple "reg".
Like registers, a field may be implemented as separate regs. For example, the register
specified using the register definition shown in Example 66, can be implemented using
the RTL code shown in Example 67. The regs named f2a_reg and f2b_reg are used to
implement field f2.
Example 66 Field Implemented with Multiple regs
register r1 @'h0010{
field f1 (f1_reg) {
reset 4'hA;
}
field f2 (f2a_reg, f2b_reg) @8 {
reset 4'h5;
}
}

Example 67 Multiple-reg- Implementation of a Fields
reg [3:0] f1_reg, f2a_reg, f2b_reg;
always @ (posedge clk)
if (rst) begin
f1_reg <= 4'hA;
{f2a_reg, f2b_reg} <= 4'h55};
end
else if (wr) case (addr)

16'h0010: begin
f1_reg <= wdat[3:0];
{f2a_reg, f2b_reg} <= wdat[11:4];
end

end

always @ (*)
if (wr) rdat = 'Z;
else case (addr)

16'h0010: rdat = {f2a_reg, f2b_reg, f1_reg};

end
A register may have a mix of read-only and writable fields.
Read-only fields cannot be written to, even with a back-door. A register containing a mix of
read-only and writable fields skip the read-only fields during a back-door write operation.
A memory must be implemented using a single unpacked array.
A memory is accessed using the offset of the memory as the index of the array storing its
content. Two memories cannot be modeled using the same array nor can a memory be
implemented using the concatenation of multiple arrays (either bit-wise or address-wise).
For example, the memory specified using the memory definition shown in Example 68,
can be implemented using the RTL code shown in Example 69. The reg named m1_reg is
used to implement the entire memory.
Example 68 Memory Specification
memory m1 (m1_reg) @'h1000{
size 1k;
bits 16;
}

Example 69 Implementation of Memory with Unpacked Array
reg [15:0] m1_reg[1024];
always @ (posedge clk)
if (wr) casex (addr)

16'b0001_00xx_xxxx_xxxx: m1_reg[addr[9:0]] <= wdat;

end

always @ (*)
if (wr) rdat = 'Z;
else casex (addr)

16'b0001_00xx_xxxx_xxxx: rdat = m1_reg[addr[9:0]];

end
Automatic generation of back-door access to memories modeled using
DesignWare models is not yet supported.
Reserved RALF Keywords in Back-door Path
ralgen error out if any RALF reserved keyword is found in any RALF back-door HDL path
specification. That means, the following RALF description in Example 70 error out.
There are two RALF keywords, block and register in the HDL path of register
reg.
Example 70 RALF Description (1)
register reg (block.register) {
…
}
If any of your RALF description has got RALF reserved keywords used in any of its back-
door HDL path specification, then use the following RALF syntax for specifying your RALF
back-door HDL path:
register reg hdl_path = (block.register) {
…
}

RAL Back-door Callbacks
The semantics of hdl_path usage is functionally/completely equivalent to the original HDL
path specification style (used in Example 70 except the fact that RALF reserved keywords
checking is disabled when the hdl_path syntax is used for specifying back-door HDL
path.
RAL Back-door Callbacks
Register Abstraction Layer (RAL) backdoor callbacks support custom behavior for register
or memory access.
To implement these callbacks, follow these steps:
1. Create a class that extends the appropriate callback façade class for register or
memory backdoor access.
2. Override one or more callback methods in your extension class to define custom read
or write behavior.
