the S1_TOP_PATH symbol must be defined to tb_top.dut, as shown in the following:
% vcs +define+S1_TOP_PATH=tb_top.dut \
ral_s1.sv
Arrays
If the RALF specification contains arrays of system, block, regfile, register, or field
instances (see “Arrays and Register Files” for more details on arrays of instances), the
hdl_path attribute must contain a %d, [%d] or [%g] format specifier.
Example 53 shows the key differences between them.
Example 53 A RALF Description Using Different Types of Array Back-door
system s1 {
block b1[2] (b1_i%d) {
register r1 (dec.r1_reg) {
field f
}
}
block b2 (blk2) {
register r2[2] (r2_array[%d]) {
field f
}
}
block b3[2] (b3_gen_array[%g].blk) {
register r3 (dec.r3_reg) {
field f
}
}
}
This section consists of the following topics:
• %d Format Specifier
• [%d] Format Specifier
• [%g] Format Specifier
%d Format Specifier
You should use the %d format specifier when the corresponding back-door RTL
implementation of the RALF array is not really an array, rather a series of similarly named
non-array signals, for example, block b1 array in Example 53.

Example 54 shows that the generated back-door path of such an RALF array does not
have any array in it.
Example 54 Generated Code During the %d Format Specifier Usage
class ral_reg_s1_b1_r1_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
int b1;

function new(int b1);
this.b1 = b1;

output bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
case (b1)
0: data = `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i0.dec.r1_reg;
1: data = `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i1.dec.r1_reg;

input bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
case (b1)
0: `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i0.dec.r1_reg = data;
1: `S1_TOP_PATH.b1_i1.dec.r1_reg = data;
An example of the RTL implementation of a RALF array, which is not an array in the back-
door/RTL, rather a series of similarly named non-array signals is as follows:
module s1();

b1 b1_i0();
b1 b1_i1();

[%d] Format Specifier
You should use the [%d] format specifier only when the end signal/variable of the
corresponding back-door RTL path/implementation is actually an array, but not a
generated instance array. For example, block r2 as shown in Example 53.

The advantage of having a normal array instead of generated instance array is that you
can access an each array element by indexing with a variable as shown in Example 55.
Example 55 Generated Code During the [%d] Format Specifier Usage
class ral_reg_s1_b2_r2_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
int r2;

output bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
data = `S1_TOP_PATH.blk2.r2_array[r2];

input bit [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] data,
`S1_TOP_PATH.blk2.r2_array[r2] = data;
[%g] Format Specifier
Use the [%g] format specifier when the corresponding back-door RTL implementation of
the RALF array is a generated instance array. For example, block b3 array as shown in
Example 56.
Because the generated instance array cannot be indexed using any variable,
numeric constants are used for indexing them.
Example 56 Generated Code During the [%g] Format specifier Usage
class ral_reg_s1_b3_r3_bkdr extends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
int b3;

tends vmm_ral_reg_backdoor;
virtual ral_oc_ethernet_intf __reg_vif;
virtual task read(output vmm_rw::status_e status, output
vmm_ral_data_t data,
input int data_id, input int
scenario_id, input int stream_id);
logic [`VMM_RAL_DATA_WIDTH-1:0] x_data;
super.pre_read(data_id, scenario_id, stream_id);
__reg_vif.ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_read(x_data);
data = x_data;
if($isunknown(x_data))
status = vmm_rw::HAS_X;
super.post_read(status, data, data_id, scenario_id,
stream_id);

virtual task write(output vmm_rw::status_e status, input
vmm_ral_data_t data,
input int data_id, input int
scenario_id, input int stream_id);
super.pre_write(data, data_id, scenario_id, stream_id);
__reg_vif.ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_write(data);

super.post_write(status, data, data_id, scenario_id,
stream_id);
The actual XMR access is placed inside the interface generated by RAL. The generated
interface is generated inside the file named ral_<top_name>_intf.sv and the generated
code is as follows:
interface ral_oc_ethernet_intf;
`include "vmm_ral.sv";
`ifdef VCS
ral_oc_ethernet_vif_container_pkg::vif_wrapper::set_vif(interface::self(
));
`endif
task ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_read(output
vmm_ral_data_t x_data);
x_data = {
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_2.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_1.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_0.DataOut
};

task ral_oc_ethernet_MODER_bkdr_write(input vmm_ral_data_t
data);
{
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_2.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_1.DataOut,
`OC_ETHERNET_TOP_PATH.ethreg1.MODER_0.DataOut
} = data;
endinterface
This allows you to place the RAL extended classes to be scoped to the SystemVerilog
package. You need to include the generated ral_<top_name>_intf.sv file during
compilation and instantiate the interface.
Target Structures
The automatically-generated back-door access code must make certain assumptions
about the nature of the HDL code used to implement the register and memory being
accessed. Although there are almost unlimited ways you can implement a register, there
are only a few styles that are supported by the back-door access generator. It is important
that, when implementing registers and memories in RTL code, a suitable coding style be
used.
The following guidelines outline the restrictions on RTL structures used to implement
registers and memories to enable automatic generation of their back-door access. Some

of these restrictions may be removed in the future as the capabilities of the back-door
access generator are improved.
If the target structures do not meet the requirements for automatic generation of back-
door access, a user-defined back-door access mechanism must be created, as specified
in User-Defined Back-doors in VMM RAL.
Writable Fields and memories must be implemented using "reg".
When performing a back-door write operation, a blocking procedural assignment is used.
This requires that the target of the assignment be a reg.
Read-only fields may be implemented using wire, parameter or Boolean expression.
Such structures cannot be written to, therefore, only the read back-door access to a read-
only field is generated. Attempting a back-door write to a read-only field results in an error.
Example 58 Read-only Field Implemented Using an Expression
always @ (*)
if (wr) rdat = 'Z;
else case (addr)

16'h0010: rdat = {fifo_fl, fifo_mt};

end
Example 59 RALF Description for Read-only Field
register r1 @'h0010{
field mt (fifo_mt) {
bits 1;
reset 1;
}
field fl (fifo_fl) {
bits 1;
reset 0;
}
}
Example 60 Alternative RALF Description for Read-only Field
register r1 (fifo_fl, fifo_mt) @'h0010{
field mt {
bits 1;
reset 1;
}

field fl {
bits 1;
reset 0;
}
}
A register may implement all of its fields in a single "reg".
A register may be composed of more than one field. All these different fields may be
implemented in the same reg that implements the overall register. This implies that all bits
in the register, up to the most-significant bits of the most-significant field, are implemented
and there are no reserved or unused bits between fields. In that case, no hdl_path should
be specified in field instantiations in the register specification.
For example, the register specified using the register definition shown in Example 61, can
be implemented using the RTL code shown in Example 62. The reg named r1_reg is used
to implement fields f1 and f2.
Example 61 Register with Multiple Fields
register r1 (r1_reg) @'h0010{
field f1 {
reset 4'hA;
}
bits 8;
reset 8'h55;
}
}
