Service Entry Point
A service entry point is invoked multiple times whenever a condition requiring software
servicing is detected. Unlike an application entry point, a service entry point always
returns, at which point it may be invoked again.
A typical service entry point is the invocation of the interrupt service code in the application
software, as illustrated in Example 91. Notice how its invocation is embedded in a forever
loop to ensure that interrupts will be serviced whenever the tb_top.int signal is asserted.
