HDL path can either be a concatenated expression of multiple RTL signals like
{ signal1, signal2, … signalN }
Or HDL path can just be one single signal like signal1.
So, the data structure representing HDL path, needed to be generic enough for being able
to support both these kinds of HDL path expressions.
The data structure chosen (for representing HDL path) is kind of a sequence of strings.
The sequence would be the same as it is in the concatenation expression. So, for the
concatenation expression shown above, the sequence would be: signal1, signal2, …
signalN.

And, for the case, where HDL path has got just one signal (no concatenation expression),
the sequence would just have one string/signal.
For either case, strings (storing the individual signals) can be accessed using this iterator
class const_hdl_path_iter.
As the nomenclature (const_*) of this iterator class indicates, this class cannot be used
for modifying the objects pointed to.
