Integrating with vmm_channel
Any scoreboard can be integrated with a monitor by sinking the output channel used to
report observed transactions. However, by sinking the output channel, no other transactor
can be connected to that same channel, often requiring the use of a vmm_broadcast
component. Example 20 shows how a scoreboard can be integrated by sinking the output
of a channel.

virtual task start();
super.start();

this.ahb.start_xactor();
ahb_tr tr;
this.ahb.out_chan.get(tr);
this.sb.expect_in_order(tr);
end
VMM channels offer the tee() method to sample the transactions flowing through it. This
method should be reserved for implementing functional coverage. The VMM Standard
Library can be optionally extended to facilitate the integration of scoreboards based
on the VMM data stream scoreboard foundation classes. The scoreboard integration
methods described below are added to the vmm_channel class when the symbol
‘VMM_SB_DS_IN_STDLIB is defined.
The extended VMM channel provides an unobtrusive mechanism that can be used to
tap the flow of transactions through a channel and forward it to a scoreboard. By simply
registering a data stream scoreboard with a channel, all transactions removed from it
will be forwarded to that scoreboard at the time of removal. Example 21 shows how this
simplifies the integration process.
Example 21 Integrating a Scoreboard by Registration with a Channel
ahb_monitor ahb;

this.ahb.out_chan.register_vmm_sb_ds(this.sb, vmm_sb_ds::EXPECT,

vmm_sb_ds::IN_ORDER);
Integrating with vmm_notify
Any scoreboard can be integrated with a notification by waiting for its indication then
sampling the associated status. Example 22 shows how a scoreboard can be integrated
by waiting for a notification to be indicated.
Example 22 Integrating a Scoreboard by Notification Indication
ahb_monitor ahb;

virtual task start();
super.start();

this.ahb.start_xactor();
this.ahb.notify.wait_for(ahb_monitor::OBSERVED);
this.sb.expect_in_order(
this.ahb.notify.status(ahb_monitor::OBSERVED));
end
Waiting for a notification indication is subject to the same event ordering and scheduling
limitation than waiting for any other SystemVerilog event has. Should a notification be
indicated multiple times within the same timestep with different status, not all indications
and status might be seen. This is usually not a problem when indications are generated by
a physical-level transactor where transaction-level events are separated in time. However,
integrating a scoreboard on a transaction-level model using notifications may cause
some reported transactions to be missed. Example 23 shows how notification indication
callbacks can be used to catch all and each indications.
Example 23 Integrating a Scoreboard by Notification Callback
