With random responses, it is no longer possible to predict the expected response strictly
from the master’s interface. It is necessary to carry the expected response from the master
to the slave to verify that the right slave is targeted with the right address and write data
and from the slave to the master (to verify the read data). This requires two scoreboards,
one in the master-to-slave direction, the other in the slave-to-master direction.
Step 2: Master-to-Slaves
The master-to-slaves scoreboard requires one queue of input transaction and three
queues of expected transaction, one per slave. This is implemented using the Data
Stream Scoreboard foundation class by defining one input stream and three expected
streams. A unique stream identifier must be assigned to each stream. The stream
identifier is chosen to facilitate the association of a slave with its corresponding stream

Step 2: Master-to-Slaves
during integration. In this case, the stream identifier corresponds to the value of ‘j’ in the
response generator thread.

`include "vmm_sb.sv"
class m2s_sb extends vmm_sb_ds;
super.new("Master->Slave");
this.define_stream(0, "Master", INPUT);
this.define_stream(0, "Slave 0", EXPECT);
this.define_stream(1, "Slave 1", EXPECT);
this.define_stream(2, "Slave 2", EXPECT);

endclass: m2s_sb

The expected transaction, as observed by the slaves, will be different from the one
injected by the master. Because each slave only has 256 addressable locations, bits
[31:8] of the address will always be zero. Any bits set by the master will be masked. Thus,
necessary to perform the same transformation in the scoreboard.

`include "vmm_sb.sv"
class m2s_sb extends vmm_sb_ds;
super.new("Master->Slave");
this.define_stream(0, "Master", INPUT);
this.define_stream(0, "Slave 0", EXPECT);
this.define_stream(1, "Slave 1", EXPECT);
this.define_stream(2, "Slave 2", EXPECT);
virtual function bit transform(input vmm_data in_pkt,
output vmm_data out_pkts[]);
apb_rw tr;
$cast(tr, in_pkt.copy());
tr.paddr[31:8] = '0;
out_pkts = new [1];
out_pkts[0] = tr;

endclass: m2s_sb

By default, the comparison function used by the Data Stream Scoreboard foundation class
is vmm_data::compare() method defined for the transaction descriptor. However, when
verifying the response of the master-to-slave path, the data value can only be compared
against an expected value for WRITE cycles. It is thus necessary to specify a custom
comparison function in the master-to-slave scoreboard. This is done by overloading the
vmm_sb_ds::compare() method.

Step 3: Integration
`include "vmm_sb.sv"
class m2s_sb extends vmm_sb_ds;
super.new("Master->Slave");
this.define_stream(0, "Master", INPUT);
this.define_stream(0, "Slave 0", EXPECT);
this.define_stream(1, "Slave 1", EXPECT);
this.define_stream(2, "Slave 2", EXPECT);
virtual function bit transform(input vmm_data in_pkt,
output vmm_data out_pkts[]);
apb_rw tr;
$cast(tr, in_pkt.copy());
tr.addr[31:8] = '0;
out_pkts = new [1];
out_pkts[0] = tr;
virtual function bit compare(vmm_data actual,
vmm_data expected);
apb_rw act, exp;
$cast(act, actual);
$cast(exp, expected);
if (act.kind == apb_rw::WRITE) begin
string diff;
return act.compare(exp, diff);
end
return (act.kind == exp.kind) &&
(act.addr == exp.addr);
endfunction: compare
endclass: m2s_sb

Step 3: Integration
Once the functionality of the scoreboard is defined, it is then integrated with the rest of
the verification environment. The stimulus transaction executed by the master can be
extracted using either the pre_cycle() or post_cycle() callback method. Since the
post_cycle() method is only invoked when the transaction is completed, pre_cycle()
method is thus the appropriate integration point.

