guaranteed. As transactions are traversed by begin time, the transaction signal
type ffrTimeBasedVCTrvsHdl also traverses signals by begin time.
When the application calls ffrTimeBasedVCTrvsHdl::ffrGotoNext
to traverse, the traverse handle always stops at a certain value change
of a certain signal if successful. The application might call methods of
ffrTimeBasedVCTrvsHdl, such as ffrGetVarIdcode, ffrGetXTag, ffrGetVC,
and ffrGetSeqNum, to get the detailed value change. For the transaction signal
type ffrTimeBasedVCTrvsHdl, the application might call transaction methods,
such as ffrGetTransInfo and ffrGetTransVal to get the detailed transaction
value change.

Transaction Traversal

It is recommended to release the time-based value change traverse handle when finished.
Otherwise, the application does not unload the value changes of the selected signals in
the traverse handle as their reference count is still not 0.

Transaction Traversal
Traversing transaction values is similar to the way the value changes of normal signals are
traversed. The only difference is that you should call ffrVCIterOne::ffrGetTransVal
or ffrVCIterOne::ffrGetTransInfo instead of ffrVCIterOne::ffrGetVC to get the
transaction value at certain time step. Note that the current time step aligns with begin
time of the transaction value. That is, transactions are traversed by begin time.
Below is a simple program that demonstrates transactions traversal:
ffr_obj->ffrAddToSignalList(stream_idcode);
ffr_obj->ffrLoadSignals();
vc_trvs_hdl = ffr_obj->ffrCreateVCTraverseHandle(stream_idcode);
if (FALSE == vc_trvs_hdl->ffrHasIncoreVC()) {
printf("This stream(%u) has no value change at all.\n",
stream_idcode);
}
