Write Design Information
This section includes the following topics:
* Idcode Scheme
* Handle Scheme
* Benefits and Limitations

Idcode Scheme
To write design information by idcode scheme, the application takes the responsibility to
make a unique idcode for each unique variable. The idcode is better to start from one so
that the maximum idcode implies how many unique variables there are in the FSDB file.
Here is the example code to demonstrate how to use idcode scheme:
struct Signal {
char *name;
int lbitnum;
int rbitnum;
fsdbVarIdcode idcode;
};
static struct Signal var_arr[2] = { {"cpu_var", 0, 0, 1}, {"ram_var", 7,
0, 2} };
ffwObject *obj;
obj = ffw_Open("test.fsdb", FSDB_FT_VERILOG);
ffw_BeginTree(obj);
ffw_CreateScope(obj, "system", FSDB_ST_VCD_MODULE);
ffw_CreateScope(obj, "cpu", FSDB_ST_VCD_MODULE);
ffw_CreateVarByIdcode(obj, FSDB_VT_VCD_WIRE, FSDB_VD_IMPLICIT,
(void*)FSDB_DT_VERILOG_STANDARD, var_arr[0].lbitnum,
var_arr[0].rbitnum,
var_arr[0].idcode, var_arr[0].name, FSDB_BYTES_PER_BIT_1B);
ffw_CreateUpscope(obj);
ffw_CreateScope(obj, "ram", FSDB_ST_VCD_MODULE);
ffw_CreateVarByIdcode(obj, FSDB_VT_VCD_WIRE, FSDB_VD_IMPLICIT,

Write Design Information

(void*)FSDB_DT_VERILOG_STANDARD, var_arr[1].lbitnum,
var_arr[1].rbitnum,
var_arr[1].idcode, var_arr[1].name, FSDB_BYTES_PER_BIT_1B);
ffw_EndTree(obj);

The above example code creates a root scope named "system," which has two subscopes named "cpu" and "ram" respectively. Scope "cpu" has a variable named "cpu_var,"
and its idcode is 1; bit size (bit-width) is also 1. Scope "ram" has a variable named
"ram_var," and its idcode is 2, bit size is 8.

Handle Scheme
To write design information by handle scheme, the application leaves the making idcode
job to the FSDB Writer. What it has to do is to keep variable's handle, that is, a pointer
to a specific in-core object. Here is the sample code to demonstrate how to use handle
scheme:
struct Signal {
char *name;
int lbitnum;
int rbitnum;
};
static struct Signal cpu = { {"cpu_var", 0, 0} };
static struct Signal ram = {{"ram_var", 7, 0} };
ffwObject *obj;
obj = ffw_Open("test.fsdb", FSDB_FT_VERILOG);
ffw_CreateTreeByHandleScheme(obj);
ffw_BeginTree(obj);
ffw_CreateScope(obj, "system", FSDB_ST_VCD_MODULE);
ffw_CreateScope(obj, "cpu", FSDB_ST_VCD_MODULE);
ffw_CreateVarByHandle(obj, FSDB_VT_VCD_WIRE, FSDB_VD_IMPLICIT,
(void*)FSDB_DT_VERILOG_STANDARD, cpu.lbitnum, cpu.rbitnum,
(void*) &cpu, cpu.name, FSDB_BYTES_PER_BIT_1B);
ffw_CreateUpscope(obj);
ffw_CreateScope(obj, "ram", FSDB_ST_VCD_MODULE);
ffw_CreateVarByHandle(obj, FSDB_VT_VCD_WIRE, FSDB_VD_IMPLICIT,
(void*)FSDB_DT_VERILOG_STANDARD, ram.lbitnum, ram.rbitnum,
(void*) &ram, ram.name, FSDB_BYTES_PER_BIT_1B);
ffw_EndTree(obj);

Instead of giving each unique variable an idcode (such as 1 and 2), now you take the
address of the variable object as the variable's handle (such as &cpu and &ram) to identify
the unique variable. The job for making an idcode for each unique variable is left to the
FSDB Writer. Note that the idcode scheme is the default scheme to create trees, so the
application must notify the FSDB Writer that it prefers to use handle scheme. Otherwise,
some necessary data structures of handle scheme are not allocated and initialized. This
notification is done by calling ffw_CreateTreeByHandleScheme.

Write Value Change

Benefits and Limitations
This section describes the benefits and limitations of idcode and handle schemes:
* Idcode Scheme: This scheme is faster. However, you must be sure that your minimum
idcode is FSDB_MIN_VAR_IDCODE and increases by 1 thereafter. In addition, you must
know which signal mapped to which idcode. That is why this scheme is easier than
handle scheme.
* Handle Scheme: This scheme is easier to use since you can use the address of a
signal as its handle and it does not have to keep track of which signal mapped to which
idcode. However, the FSDB Writer does the handle -> idcode mapping and therefore
this scheme is less efficient than idcode scheme.
If you are focusing on performance or if you already encode the signals just as your idcode
scheme does, you must use idcode scheme. On the other hand, if the performance is not
your priority (actually, the impact on performance is generally little) or if you do not want to
handle the trouble of encoding the signals as the idcode scheme does, then you can use
handle scheme.

